VT_TALK("sql/sql-modify", {
 "insert": "Tables start empty and grow one insert at a time. You write INSERT INTO, the table name, the list of columns you are filling, and then the values in the same order. Columns you leave out get their default value, or NULL. One statement can add many rows, each in its own pair of brackets. INSERT can also take its rows from a SELECT, which is how people copy or archive data, for example building a restock list from every product that has run out. Always write the list of columns, because a statement without it breaks quietly the day someone adds a new column. Think of filling a new row in a register, writing each value under the right heading.",
 "update": "UPDATE changes values in rows that already exist, and DELETE removes rows. Both use WHERE to choose the rows, and here is the most important warning in this course. Without WHERE, they change or remove every row in the table. In the animation, choose a change and a WHERE test, and watch which rows are marked before anything happens. Then clear the WHERE box and see the whole table change. The safe habit is to write the WHERE as a SELECT first, check the rows, and only then turn it into an update or delete. When rows have children, such as orders with order lines, delete the children first. Think of a teacher who reads out the names on a list before striking any of them off.",
 "upsert": "A very common job is to add a row, but update it if it already exists. That is called an upsert, update or insert. The engine needs a unique or primary key column to notice the clash. In SQLite and PostgreSQL you write INSERT, then ON CONFLICT, then DO UPDATE or DO NOTHING. Inside the update, the word excluded means the row you tried to insert. MySQL writes ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE, and SQL Server and Oracle use MERGE. RETURNING hands back the rows a statement just changed, so an app gets the new id without asking again. Think of a shop counter where a known customer gets points added to their card, and a new customer gets a fresh card.",
 "fkact": "When a parent row is deleted, the foreign key decides what happens to its children. The default, no action, simply refuses the delete while children still point at the parent. CASCADE deletes the children too, which suits order lines or photos that mean nothing on their own. SET NULL keeps the children but clears their link, like players whose team was disbanded. You write the action on the child column, after REFERENCES. In SQLite these rules only work when foreign keys are switched on, and every block on this site switches them on for you. Think of deleting a photo album on your phone, and every photo inside it going with it.",
 "levels": "Here is the ladder for this chapter. For a beginner, insert adds, update changes and delete removes, and WHERE says which rows. At college level, these statements are called data manipulation language, and each statement is atomic, so if one row breaks a rule, the whole statement is undone. At masters level, upserts depend on unique indexes to stay correct when many users write at once. At research level, an update inside the engine is often a delete plus an insert, and PostgreSQL keeps old row versions until a cleaner called VACUUM removes them. Think of a librarian who keeps the old card in a drawer until nobody needs it."
});
